[Music]  [Music]  it's a name that needs no introduction  it's a series not much younger than  video games themselves and by now you've  probably heard breath of the wild the  newest century in the story pantheon of  the Nintendo's iconic franchise is not  just good its revolutionary it redefines  what a Zelda game can be removing three  decades of tradition with both minor  mechanics and major overarching system  and it single-handedly rekindled a  Nintendo flame that's slowly been fading  for years now but breath of the wild is  also a massive open world game and any  video game landscape replete with  formulaic retreads of the same go here  do this structure we've come to expect  in these titles this new Zelda could not  have arrived at a better time  as I make my way across this treacherous  new Hyrule I can't shake the feeling  that everything is about to change  [Music]  maybe  [Music]  understand why breath of the wild is a  benchmark for open-world games it's  probably helpful to first look at its  significance in the Zelda series the  Zelda formula has long been about  finding tools which act as keys to new  areas funneling you from one space to  another  you also descend into dungeons these  discrete locations with environmental  puzzles a new piece of equipment and a  boss that tests your skill with set  equipment once you've completed enough  dungeons you can travel to the game's  final boss usually some form of the evil  king Ganon and beat the game the  structure has always worked and has led  to some amazing games but it's gotten  old when the original Zelda released in  1986 it was a testament to what video  games could do they could transport us  to another place when we could explore  on a whim where we could venture off the  beaten path of 2d side-scroller x' and  confined interior settings it's ironic  but up until breadth of the wild the  Zelda game that offered you the most  freedom was the first Zelda game they  capture the joy of pure exploration  venturing into the woods out of  curiosity crossing a river to see what  lies on the opposite bank the Nintendo  Entertainment System technical  limitations ensured that you really  weren't seeing all that much but you  fleshed out the rest with your  imagination then the Nintendo 64 release  and we realized how deeply console games  could communicate a sense of place Super  Mario 64 paves the way for fantastic  games in Nintendo's 3d space offering up  a platform that still stands today is  one of the best it followed in the wake  of PC titles approved we could expand  the ways we looked at games and how we  design levels to create a more engaging  experience for the first time on console  we could really feel the geography we  could really feel the movement we could  look into the distance and say let's go  there but then along came Ocarina of  Time it dropped us into a world of  forest fields massive lakes and rolling  desert as opposed to Mario 64 separate  levels ocarina tide all of its locales  together into one cohesive world for  many reminded them of their time  exploring the original Legend of Zelda  too many ocarina was the idealized  version of that first game it was the  version they saw in their minds i when  Nintendo is Hardware limited what they  were seeing on screen but not what they  imagined it to be  ocarina was a revolution for Nintendo  the Zelda series and video games in  general and now breadth of the wild is  here and I can't stop seeing the  parallels between the two  you  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  I was born after the release of the  original Legend of Zelda so like many  players Ocarina was the first time I  felt truly transported it was escapism  yes but it was also first time I  realized that many of the stories we  pull from games are the stories we tell  ourselves I didn't just go to the spirit  temple I cross a blistery sandstorm  fought discussing enemies that rose from  the ground met a ghost who showed me the  way and I realized I could only progress  if I return to the same spot seven years  in the past breath of the wild is the  embodiment of stories like these it's  about the time I barely scale the  slippery cliff in the pouring rain it's  about that time I pulled an enemy arrow  for my shield loaded my bow and fired it  right back it's about that traveler I  met on the road who I never saw again  all of this is to say that for the first  time my wish for what a Zelda could be  had finally aligned with what it really  is I don't have to imagine the grand  adventure here Nintendo had shown it to  me the breath of the wild doesn't just  overshadow every other Zelda game it  overshadows every other open-world game  yes even Metal Gear Solid 5 yes even The  Witcher 3 and yes even Grand Theft Auto  5 horizon zero dawn released the week  before breath of the wild and it is an  excellent game it ties nature and  technology together into a memorable  world and it tells a great story about  the humans of the far future it also  introduces robot hunting mechanics to  set it combat apart from other  third-person titles for a while I  absolutely loved it but then I returned  to it after playing breast of the wild  and something fell off something had  changed I was following my waypoints I  was gathering plans and branches and  machine parts I was hunting massive  mechanical beef but I was just going  through the motions after playing  breadth of the wild horizons world had  lost my interest  that's because breath of the wild uses  its every mechanic it's every  overlapping system to create a more  meaningful sense of place the wilderness  isn't something we just traversed here  it's something we need to survive it's  world map isn't just an arena in which  we fight numerous enemies Hyrule itself  is an enemy to rest of the wild  introduces its survival mechanics within  several moments of link waking up and  walking out into the world  this is Lightspeed by today's standards  where games hold our hand through the  first few hours ensuring that we  understand exactly how much  and exactly how we're supposed to use  them in the future  Zelda games have been guilty of this for  decades but here we are in a Zelda game  and five minutes after the opening  cinematic we are already seeing the  complex cause-and-effect scenarios of  breath of the wild mechanics chain  reaction events usually reserved for the  late game hours of lesser title I  [Music]  originally spoke to Todd Howard designer  a fallout 4 and Elder Scrolls 5 about  what makes a good open-world game and he  said that the best one say yes to the  player more often they allow the player  not just the freedom to explore but the  freedom to tinker with the world on a  whim breath of the wild says yes to the  player all the time  can I climb that cliff yes can I chop  down that tree to make a bridge across  this ravine  yes can I steal the weapons off that  sleeping giants necklace before he wakes  up and chases me across a nearby bridge  of course but just being able to do  these things isn't what makes pres of  the wild so special it's the fact that  these actions are all essential to its  world because this is a harsh world it's  a survival game where the climate your  stamina and your supplies are all major  factors you cook meals to boost your  energy and Don warm clothes to withstand  the cold it's survival mechanics demand  that you both rely on and remain wary of  the wilderness the difficulty of your  enemies on the other hand encourages you  to explore Hyrule every nook and cranny  hoping for better gear to make you  stronger in some cases you'll encounter  foes too strong to handle in your  current state hours later you're better  equipped and better prepared to return  and take your revenge  Hyrule friendly inhabitants are  essential in the open world as well  offering more than just backstory or the  sense of a living world townfolk offer  up rumors warnings or ghost stories that  lead you into the remote places of  Hyrule you explore more StumbleUpon  campfire sneak around it before  declaring it safe this is all an endless  rewarding loop that ties every facet of  the world together  you have an overarching objective but  the simple story contextualizes things  so you don't feel guilty for wandering  the story supports the idea of a lost  hero living off the land linking up with  other champions growing stronger before  finally venturing into the castle in the  distance I'm not just foraging for the  hell of it or moving from Waypoint to  Waypoint I mean this in all honesty but  after all the hours I put into breath of  the wild there is still never a dull  moment and that's the key thing here in  breath of the wild I am always thinking  even as I make my way across a flat  filled with almost no focal points in  sight I'm thinking five steps ahead how  many arrows do I have do I need to make  camp and cook food how close is this  weapon to degrading what happens at beau  Cobb when cavalry attacked me out of  nowhere should I head for those cliffs  to get out of the open this is all to  say that breath of the wild is an open  world not just for the sake of it  because it's a major trend in recent  years its open-world because Nintendo  wanted to impart the feeling of being on  a dangerous adventure and every system  at play here feeds into that idea this  is a massive Hyrule we're exploring but  there is not much filler at all  I love The Witcher 3 is branching web of  storylines I love the absurdity and  freedom of Grand Theft Auto 5 I love the  immersion moments as Metal Gear Solid 5  but I think we've all grown weary of  open-world games in recent years we've  become tired of comparing map sizes or  checking off boxes on an ever growing  list of fetch quest but breast is a wild  like ocarina of time before it or The  Legend of Zelda before that came along  to upset the status quo a came at a time  when video games were oversaturated with  open worlds and we all forgot what made  them so magical to begin with been a  while since a Legend of Zelda game as  challenge appears but breath of a while  just arrived and it brought clear  message open world games can be  interesting again  [Music]  me  hey everyone thanks so much for watching  our second episode of reboot on breath  of the wild you haven't yet like and  subscribe it helps us keep doing these  and we really enjoy doing them it is a  biweekly show and in the off week we do  a Q&amp;A in our past episode as well as  teaser next episode so get your YouTube  comments in tweet at Jake Decker or me  we might feature your comments on the  show in our off weeks so get those in  and we're looking forward to seeing you

